@model Diten.Web.Models.TaskTypes.TaskTypeClosureOutcomeRow
@using Diten.Web.Views.Tasks.TaskTypes
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Localization
@using Diten.Web.Views.WorkCenterNext
@inject IHtmlLocalizer<TaskTypesIndex> Localizer
@* ⚠ THE SYSTEM OUTCOME LABELS COME FROM THE WORKCENTERNEXT RESX, NOT THIS ONE.
   `WorkAggregation_ClosureOutcome_*` already exists there in all seven languages — the projection emits those
   keys and the Task Center resolves them. Copying the five entries into TaskTypesIndex would put the SAME
   sentence in two files, and the day one is corrected the two screens would disagree about what an outcome is
   called. So the second localizer is injected instead. *@
@inject IHtmlLocalizer<WorkCenterNextIndex> OutcomeLocalizer
@{
    var outcome = Model.Outcome;
    var key = Model.RowKey;
    var isSystem = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(outcome.LabelResourceKey);
}
@*
    ONE ROW OF THE CLOSURE OUTCOME DICTIONARY — declared ONCE.

    _Form renders this for every stored row AND once inside a <template> with the placeholder key, so the markup
    an administrator edits and the markup the Add button clones are the same file. Two copies of a row is how the
    cloned one quietly loses a field name and stops binding.

    The visual shape is Sneat's repeater row (forms-extras.html:1955 — `data-repeater-list` / `data-repeater-item`):
    a `.row` of `col-*` inputs with the delete control on the right. ⚠ MEASURED: `jquery.repeater`, which drives
    those pages, IS vendored (wwwroot/assets/vendor/libs/jquery-repeater/) but NOTHING WIRES IT — no view loads the
    script, and its only caller is app-ecommerce-product-add.js, a Sneat sample no view references. Copying the
    `data-repeater-*` markup would therefore have produced an Add button that silently does nothing. The look is
    borrowed; the behaviour is a small vanilla script. (Wiring the vendor library instead remains open — it is
    present, not absent.)
*@
<div class="row g-3 align-items-end mb-3" data-closure-outcome-row>
    @*
        NON-SEQUENTIAL INDEX BINDING. `<name>.Index` lets the model binder accept arbitrary keys, so removing the
        middle row does not require renumbering the ones after it — which a clone-and-append editor cannot do
        without re-rendering the whole list and throwing away what the user has typed.
    *@
    <input type="hidden" name="ClosureOutcomes.Index" value="@key" />

    @* Identifies a SYSTEM outcome. Hidden because it is not something anyone types: it is the catalogue's key,
       bound to the seven translations this product ships. Empty for a tenant outcome. *@
    <input type="hidden" name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].LabelResourceKey"
           value="@outcome.LabelResourceKey" data-closure-outcome-key />

    <div class="col-12 col-lg-3">
        <label class="form-label fw-medium">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeSource"]</label>
        <select class="form-select" data-closure-outcome-source>
            @* "Custom" is a REAL CHOICE — the administrator writing their own outcome — not a placeholder, so it
               says so in words rather than showing a greyed prompt. *@
            <option value="">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeSourceCustom"]</option>
            @foreach (var system in Model.SystemOutcomes)
            {
                var systemKey = system.LabelResourceKey ?? system.Code;
                <option value="@system.Code"
                        data-disposition="@system.Disposition"
                        data-requires-reason="@(system.RequiresReason ? "true" : "false")"
                        data-resource-key="@system.LabelResourceKey"
                        data-label="@OutcomeLocalizer[systemKey].Value"
                        selected="@(isSystem && string.Equals(outcome.Code, system.Code, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))">
                    @OutcomeLocalizer[systemKey]
                </option>
            }
        </select>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12 col-md-4 col-lg-2">
        <label class="form-label fw-medium">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeCode"]</label>
        @* A system row's code belongs to the catalogue. `readonly`, NEVER `disabled`: a disabled input posts
           nothing, and this code is the value stored on every task closed this way. *@
        <input type="text" class="form-control" maxlength="40"
               name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].Code" value="@outcome.Code"
               readonly="@isSystem"
               placeholder="@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeCodePlaceholder"]"
               data-closure-outcome-code />
    </div>

    <div class="col-12 col-md-8 col-lg-3">
        <label class="form-label fw-medium">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeLabel"]</label>
        @*
            TWO CONTROLS, EXACTLY ONE VISIBLE — the label split TaskFieldDefinition established.

            The SYSTEM control is read-only and carries NO `name`, so it never posts: the resource key alone
            identifies the outcome and the translation already exists in all seven languages. The TENANT control
            posts the author's own words, in the one language they typed them in.

            Posting both would be REFUSED by the server (OutcomeLabelAmbiguousMessage), which is why the script
            clears the text box when a system outcome is chosen rather than merely hiding it.
        *@
        <input type="text" class="form-control @(isSystem ? "" : "d-none")" readonly
               value="@(isSystem ? OutcomeLocalizer[outcome.LabelResourceKey!].Value : string.Empty)"
               data-closure-outcome-system-label />
        <input type="text" class="form-control @(isSystem ? "d-none" : "")" maxlength="160"
               placeholder="@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeLabelPlaceholder"]"
               name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].LabelText"
               value="@(isSystem ? string.Empty : outcome.LabelText)"
               data-closure-outcome-text />
    </div>

    <div class="col-6 col-md-4 col-lg-2">
        <label class="form-label fw-medium">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeDisposition"]</label>
        @* CODE values that travel, never translated words — the same rule RecordClass follows above. *@
        <select class="form-select" name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].Disposition" data-closure-outcome-disposition>
            <option value="Completed" selected="@(outcome.Disposition == "Completed")">@Localizer["ClosureDispositionCompleted"]</option>
            <option value="Cancelled" selected="@(outcome.Disposition == "Cancelled")">@Localizer["ClosureDispositionCancelled"]</option>
        </select>
    </div>

    <div class="col-6 col-md-4 col-lg-1">
        <label class="form-label fw-medium">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeSortOrder"]</label>
        <input type="number" class="form-control" min="0"
               name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].SortOrder" value="@outcome.SortOrder" />
    </div>

    <div class="col-8 col-md-3 col-lg-2">
        @*
            ⭐ PER ROW, and that is the design rather than a convenience. A single "notes are mandatory" switch
            above the list forces a sentence onto the outcomes that do not need one; those boxes fill with "ok",
            and the field then carries no signal on the outcomes that DID need it. "Rejected" asks why,
            "Approved" does not — one list, two answers.
        *@
        <div class="form-check mb-2">
            @* The hidden false is what makes UNCHECKING post at all — an unchecked checkbox sends nothing, so
               without it a box could be ticked and never untucked. *@
            @*
                ⚠ CHECKBOX FIRST, HIDDEN SECOND — AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE RULE.

                MVC binds the FIRST value it meets for a repeated name. Written the other way round, a ticked box
                puts "false,true" on the wire and the binder takes "false": the switch can never be turned on, and
                nothing complains. MEASURED live on this very row before it was fixed — FormData(form) returned
                ["false","true"] with the box ticked.

                This cannot be an `asp-for` checkbox: that emits its hidden partner into FormContext.EndOfFormContent
                at </form>, which the <template> this partial also renders is not part of. So the pair is written by
                hand here, and the order is the only thing keeping it honest. A test asserts it; do not reorder.
            *@
            <input type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].RequiresReason"
                   value="true" checked="@outcome.RequiresReason" data-closure-outcome-requires-reason />
            <input type="hidden" name="ClosureOutcomes[@key].RequiresReason" value="false" />
            <label class="form-check-label small">@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeRequiresReason"]</label>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-4 col-md-1 col-lg-1 text-end">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-label-danger" data-closure-outcome-remove
                aria-label="@Localizer["ClosureOutcomeRemove"]">
            <i class="bx bx-trash"></i>
        </button>
    </div>
</div>
